  <content>
  <section><title>Distributive Property </title>
<div>
  <para> <emphasis> Distributive Property</emphasis></para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\), \(b\), \(c\) are real numbers, then:</para>
  <para>\(a(b+c)=ab+ac\)</para>
  <para>Other forms:</para>
  <para>\(a(b-c)=ab-ac\)</para>
  <para>\((b+c)a=ba+ca\)</para>
</div>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>Simplify \(3(x + 4)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute the 3 to both the \(x\) and the 4.<figure><media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING 3 MULTIPLIED BY X PLUS 4. AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 3 TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 3 TO 4."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e2748adb9c598d3b797ef94366a2dd928d0839c8"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the terms.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>Simplify \(m(7 - 2m)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute the \(m\) to both the 7 and the \(2m\).<figure><media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING M TIMES 7 MINUS 2M. AN ARROW IS GOING FROM M TO 7. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM M TO 2M."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ebc4a07d0e803af953b7750529168c66bb4fd906"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the terms. Recall \(m \cdot m=m^2\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
<para>Simplify \(5x(x-\frac{2}{5})\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute the \(5x\) to both the \(x\) and the \(\frac25\).<figure><media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING 5X TIMES X MINUS TWO-FIFTHS. AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 5X TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 5X TO TWO-FIFTHS."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b6ef0597bbf9b2977d388df05b7c406d236b877b"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the terms. Recall \(x \cdot x=x^2\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
<title>Try It: Distributive Property</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Simplify \(7y(4y-\frac{3}{7})\)</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to simplify the expression using the distributive property:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute the \(7y\) to both the \(4y\) and the \(\frac{3}{7}\) .</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING 7Y TIMES 4Y MINUS THREE-SEVENTHS. AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 7Y TO 4Y. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 7Y TO THREE-SEVENTHS."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fe0e11e96f07d66407c65c909143784ea5b4aa92"/></media></figure></para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the terms. Recall \(y \cdot y=y^2\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
